Spring Pulsar to connect to a broker over `pulsar://host:6650` — here's the decision.\n\nKey findings:\n- `pulsar-client-all` is a self-contained uber-jar; its POM pulls in `pulsar-client-api`, `bouncy-castle-bc`, BouncyCastle, OpenTelemetry, slf4j, etc. as normal transitive Maven deps — Gradle resolves these automatically, so no extra `dependency` entry is needed (same as the existing `io.nats:jnats` and `com.rabbitmq.client` scaffolds, which add only the library coordinate itself).\n- Meaningful reachability coverage for a messaging client means actually creating a producer/consumer against a live broker — this repo's convention for exactly that situation (`io.nats:jnats`, `com.rabbitmq.client:amqp-client`) is a `required-docker-images.txt` + a `docker run` in the test, backed by a pinned tag under `tests/tck-build-logic/src/main/resources/allowed-docker-images/`. No Pulsar image is pinned there yet.\n- The official `apachepulsar/pulsar` image runs a full standalone broker (+ BookKeeper + ZooKeeper in one process) via `bin/pulsar standalone`, exposing `6650` (binary protocol) and `8080` (admin HTTP). Tag `4.2.5` exists on Docker Hub and matches the client version exactly (verified against Maven Central's `maven-metadata.xml`, which also confirms `pulsar-client-all:4.2.5` itself is published).\n- Pulsar standalone is noticeably heavier/slower to boot than `nats`/`rabbitmq`, so the generated test needs a readiness-poll loop (not a fixed sleep) and should disable functions-worker/stream-storage to cut startup cost — this is test-code guidance, not a deterministic setup item.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"advisory_preparation\",\n  \"summary\": \"pulsar-client-all needs a live broker for meaningful producer/consumer coverage; no extra Maven dependency required since the uber-jar resolves its own transitives.\",\n  \"deterministic_setup\": [\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"docker_image\",\n      \"image\": \"apachepulsar/pulsar:4.2.5\",\n      \"slug\": \"apachepulsar_pulsar\",\n      \"reason\": \"pulsar-client-all is a network client; exercising producer/consumer/admin paths requires a running Pulsar broker, matching this repo's existing pattern for io.nats:jnats and com.rabbitmq.client:amqp-client. Tag 4.2.5 matches the library version exactly.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"agent_guidance\": \"Start the container with `bin/pulsar standalone --no-functions-worker --no-stream-storage` to avoid starting the functions worker and BookKeeper-backed stream storage, which aren't needed for basic producer/consumer coverage and would otherwise slow startup. Expose ports 6650 (client serviceUrl `pulsar://localhost:6650`) and 8080 (admin HTTP, usable for readiness polling, e.g. GET /admin/v2/clusters). Pulsar standalone takes substantially longer to become ready than nats/rabbitmq (it boots a broker, bookie, and ZooKeeper in one JVM), so the test must poll/retry for readiness with a generous timeout rather than reuse the short fixed sleep pattern seen in JnatsTest. Tear down the container (docker kill/rm) after the test so repeated local runs don't leak containers.\",\n  \"risks\": [\n    \"Pulsar standalone's startup is slower and more resource-hungry than the other broker images already allow-listed (nats, rabbitmq), which may increase test wall-clock time and flakiness risk under constrained CI resources.\",\n    \"pulsar-client-all is a large shaded jar (~hundreds of MB transitively: netty, avro, protobuf, bouncycastle); native-image build time/memory could be heavier than typical libraries in this repo — no specific blocking GraalVM issue was found during research, but local Gradle/native-image verification remains authoritative.\"\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms":9022,"type":"result","duration_ms":230221,"uuid":"73683c41-408f-4ffe-8b19-427d4a462c9a","ttft_stream_ms":1797,"time_to_request_ms":101,"first_content_frame_ms":1797,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

